            <ArticleTitle>Radial access - be prepared!</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>549-50</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We present the case of a 46-year-old female patient that experienced recurrent stent thrombosis and underwent attempted primary PCI via the radial artery. Although radial artery cannulation with a 6 Fr sheath and the diagnostic angiography were successfully performed, the PCI could not be achieved because of failure to advance the 6 Fr guide through the radial artery, due to severe angulation of a high take-off radial artery, combined with a proximal radial artery stenosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
